import sys
from pathlib import Path
TD=Path(__file__).resolve().parent;sys.path.insert(0,str(TD.parent))
from manuscript_support import build
E=[
('01-00','Conditions for health','Clean water, hygiene, an adequate diet, prevention and treatment of disease, and social conditions can influence health and life expectancy.','Life expectancy is an average under the mortality conditions used, not an individual promise. Poverty can restrict food, housing and service access; stress and illness can interact with those constraints.'),
('01-01','Water and hygiene','Safe water and appropriate hygiene reduce exposure to some contamination pathways.','A supply needs reliable access and protection after collection; a new water point does not help equally if distance, breakdown or unsafe handling prevents use.'),
('01-02','Adequate diet','A nutritionally adequate diet provides suitable energy and nutrients for health.','Availability, affordability and varied intake matter. More food by weight does not necessarily establish adequate nutrition, and a household label does not diagnose nutritional status.'),
('01-03','Disease prevention and care','Prevention and effective accessible care can reduce illness and its consequences.','Facilities, trained staff, supplies and timely referral must work together. A building without operating support does not establish useful health provision.'),
('01-04','Poverty and stress','Material hardship can limit adequate food, housing and service access, while insecurity and stress can affect well-being.','Illness can also reduce earnings and add care costs. Explain interacting pathways without assuming poverty establishes an individual’s health or assigning blame.'),
('02-00','Evaluate health programmes','Primary health care links accessible care, prevention and community needs; hospitals, trained doctors and supporting workers provide complementary capabilities.','Trained auxiliaries need suitable roles, supervision and referral support; immunisation, community programmes and safe-water provision address different needs. Compare accessible coverage, quality, staffing, maintenance and cost rather than simply counting buildings.'),
('03-00','HIV and development','HIV-related illness can affect work, household care and public resources in Namibia and other SADC economies, while effective care improves opportunities to remain well and participate.','Accessible testing, treatment and support form part of a public-health response. Avoid treating HIV status as incapacity or using a fictional illness series as actual national prevalence evidence.'),
('03-01','Economic pathways','Illness or premature death can reduce available labour, increase caring work and divert household resources.','Treatment access and support can reduce these burdens; stigma and exclusion can add avoidable economic costs independently of health status.'),
('03-02','System responses','HIV responses can combine prevention information, confidential testing, ongoing treatment, maternal-child services and community support.','Continuity, trained services and non-stigmatising access matter. Individual treatment decisions belong with qualified health professionals, not a classroom model answer.')]
L={s:(m,e) for s,h,m,e in E};Q={}
def q(n,p,a,g):Q[n]=(p,a,g)
q(1,'Explain why a health-service building alone does not establish improved health access.','Trained staff, supplies and usable access must allow it to function. A building without them adds physical capacity without necessarily delivering care.',['Reason: connect the missing operating support to usable care.'])
q(2,'Explain the access mechanism in an invented village with a safe water point that repeatedly breaks and households then collecting from an unprotected source.','Breakdowns remove reliable safe access and push collection towards a less protected source. Maintenance addresses the reliability link, rather than counting the installed point as continuous coverage.',['Mechanism: combine breakdown and alternative-source facts.','Consequence: reduced usable safe access; no invented disease rate.'])
q(3,'Calculate percentage change where fictional service attendance rises from 80 to 100. Show working.','100 - 80 = 20; 20 ÷ 80 = 0.25; multiplying by 100 gives a 25% increase.',['Working: difference divided by original attendance, converted to percent.','Unit: percent increase, not percentage points.'])
q(14,'Explain why a rise from 80 to 100 users is not a 20% increase.','Twenty is the absolute difference. Divide it by the original 80 and multiply by 100 to obtain 25%; test whether the original-value denominator has been used.',['Error: absolute difference treated as percentage change.','Distinction: count increase versus relative increase.'])
for n,s in [(4,'01-01'),(5,'01-02'),(6,'01-03'),(7,'01-04')]:q(n,'Explain one health-development connection concerning '+dict((x[0],x[1]) for x in E)[s].lower()+'.',' '.join(L[s]),['Reason: '+L[s][1]])
q(8,'Explain which fictional programme better fits remote basic-care access: A builds a distant hospital without transport or staff funding; B supports accessible primary care with trained staff, supplies and referral arrangements.','B more directly addresses basic access because operating resources and referrals are included. A may offer specialist potential, but distance and unfunded staffing limit current use; hospitals and primary care are complementary rather than interchangeable.',['Judgement: compare against remote basic-care access.','Reason: weigh practical delivery and specialist potential using the actual constraints.'])
q(9,'Describe this original fictional comparison: A has two facilities and 40% of residents able to reach staffed care; B has one facility plus outreach and 75% access.','B has fewer buildings but access 35 percentage points higher. The access pattern shows why facility counts alone are insufficient evidence of usable coverage.',['Evidence: compare facility counts and access percentages.','Relationship: contrast installed infrastructure and delivered access.'])
q(10,'Explain the economic pathway in an invented household where illness interrupts paid work and another member reduces hours to provide care, while savings are limited.','Lost paid hours reduce income while care limits a second earner’s time. Limited savings make the combined loss harder to absorb; continued health and social support could address the burden without excluding affected people.',['Mechanism: combine labour and care-time effects.','Consequence: household income/resource pressure under supplied conditions.'])
q(11,'Explain which fictional HIV-support option better fits continued participation: accessible confidential care with transport support, or exclusion from work despite the person being able to perform the job.','Care and transport support address an actual health-access barrier while preserving productive opportunity. Exclusion removes earnings without a capacity reason and can add stigma and household pressure.',['Judgement: choose against health access and continued participation.','Reason: weigh support and the unsupported exclusion; no clinical prescription is required.'])
for n,s in [(12,'03-01'),(13,'03-02')]:q(n,'Describe two aspects of '+dict((x[0],x[1]) for x in E)[s].lower()+'.',' '.join(L[s]),['Feature: '+L[s][0],'Feature: '+L[s][1]])
P={'P01':(['01-00','02-00'],'State (a) two supports needed to turn a clinic building into usable care [2]. Explain (b) a fictional programme where a new clinic is built but no staff or medicine supply is funded and travel remains unaffordable [4].','(a) Trained staff and dependable supplies. (b) The building does not deliver operating care without staff and supplies, while unaffordable travel limits access even if it opens. Implementation must address operating capacity and reach together.',['Point: two valid operating supports [2].','Development: connect both missing capacity and access to the bounded programme result [4].']),
'P02':(['01-00','02-00'],'Calculate (a) usable service coverage if 180 of 300 eligible residents can reach staffed care in a fictional district [2]. Explain (b) whether a second unstaffed building alone solves the gap when the unserved residents also lack transport [4].','(a) 180 ÷ 300 = 0.6; multiplying by 100 gives 60%. (b) Forty percent remain uncovered, but an unstaffed building adds no operating care and does not remove transport barriers. Prioritise feasible staffing and access arrangements rather than building counts alone.',['Evidence: correct denominator and 60% [2].','Meaning: combine uncovered share, absent staff and transport to justify the practical priority [4].'])}
examples=[(n,'Follow the mechanism',Q[k][0]+' '+Q[k][1],[s for s in L if s.startswith(n+'-')]) for n,k in [('01',2),('02',8),('03',10)]]
examples.append(('02','Programme evidence and limits','[WHO’s primary-health-care overview](https://www.who.int/health-topics/primary-health-care) describes community-centred services and broader health conditions. Its [2024 Namibia account](https://www.afro.who.int/news/namibia-reaches-key-milestone-eliminating-mother-child-transmission-hiv-and-hepatitis-b) reports integration of maternal-child and primary health services and progress on reducing vertical transmission. These show linked programmes, not proof that every household is served. Checked 20 October 2026; exact clinical procedures and schedules are outside this lesson.',['02-00','03-00','03-02']))
examples.append(('03','Use accurate HIV language','[WHO’s HIV fact sheet](https://www.who.int/en/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/hiv-aids), checked 20 October 2026, explains that treatment can control HIV and that ordinary contact such as sharing food does not transmit it. Do not use a diagnosis as a reason to exclude someone from schooling or work. This lesson does not prescribe individual treatment.',['03-00','03-01','03-02']))
links={n:'Connect to Population (7.1), Development (1.1) and Education (7.3). Separate installed facilities, usable coverage and health outcomes. Synthetic figures model reasoning and are not current Namibian statistics.' for n in ['01','02','03']}
if __name__=='__main__':build(TD,E,Q,P,links,examples)
